## Rules / Never do
- Read CLAUDE.md, DECISIONS.md, and SESSIONS.md at the start of every session and stay anchored to them (this is enforced by a SessionStart hook — see `.claude/settings.json` — but state it here too as the source of truth)
- Verify external claims (API behavior, docs, third-party task briefs) against live/actual behavior before trusting them and writing code against them — a docs page or a brief being plausible is not the same as it being correct right now. This applies to your own verification too: don't report a check, scan, or test suite as clean without evidence it's actually capable of catching the thing it claims to catch — a check that would pass even with the defect present gives false confidence, and re-running it any number of times won't reveal that. Where practical, prove a check can fail before trusting a pass from it (inject a known instance of the defect class it claims to catch and confirm it's caught); where that's not practical, say the check is judgment-based rather than letting it borrow the credibility of a mechanical one.
- Anything that must never be violated belongs in this file, not only in a file this one merely points at. Other documents (a standards doc, a design doc) may elaborate on a rule, but a rule that exists *only* in a non-auto-loaded file is a rule the session has to remember to go open a second file to find — the exact failure mode the SessionStart hook exists to prevent for this file itself. If a standard is truly absolute, restate it here even at the cost of duplication; duplicating one absolute rule costs a few lines, while a rule that lives only in the wrong place costs the rule.
- When a unit of work touches input capture (keystrokes, clipboard, screen/audio recording), accessibility APIs, credentials, or personal data, state its security/privacy posture explicitly before calling it done — what's captured, what's deliberately excluded, and where consent or suppression applies. A brief that's silent on this is not permission to build the most capable/invasive version by default; if the brief doesn't say, ask rather than assume. This is a lighter, author-side complement to `qc-review`, not a replacement for it — `qc-review` is a single adversarial pass at the end, and this rule exists so the question gets asked while the work is being built, not only after.
- Use plan mode (or otherwise get explicit sign-off) before non-trivial implementation work — multiple valid approaches, multi-file changes, or unclear requirements all qualify
- Commit only when explicitly asked; never use destructive git operations (force-push, hard reset, history rewrites) without explicit confirmation
- Always read a file before editing it; if an exact-match replacement fails, re-read the file to find the actual current content rather than reaching for a regex fallback
- Proactively suggest a checkpoint when EITHER (a) the session has covered several unrelated subtasks or run long, OR (b) the working directory has changed to a genuinely different project than where the session started (e.g. you began in project A, then `cd`'d into unrelated project B and kept working). Trigger (b) is a stronger, more specific signal than (a) and should not wait for (a) to also be true — a conversation that has drifted across multiple unrelated project directories is exactly when stale/cross-project context bleed is most likely, and each project's docs should be checkpointed before moving on. To checkpoint: update SESSIONS.md (and memory, if available) with current state, then suggest the user consider `/clear` before continuing — don't wait to be asked. A fresh session re-anchors on CLAUDE.md/DECISIONS.md/SESSIONS.md via the SessionStart hook rather than accumulated conversational drift, which is the actual mechanism that keeps long-running work reliable — not a fixed context-percentage target.
- End a turn with a structured question (`AskUserQuestion`) offering 2-4 concrete next steps, rather than an open-ended "let me know what you'd like next" — picking is materially cheaper than composing a reply, and by the end of a turn the assistant usually knows the real candidates better than the user does. Don't add a catch-all option; "Other" is supplied by the tool. Three limits, all of which matter more than the rule itself: (1) if the next step is genuinely obvious, just do it and say so — this rule never overrides "pick the sensible default and proceed," and a menu of filler options costs the exact round-trip it exists to save; (2) if the assistant has an actual recommendation, it must be one of the options and labeled as such — a recommendation left in the prose above the question reads as commentary next to a menu that defines what the outcome can be, so it is effectively not on the table (compressed from Foundry's own playbook entry 9c-iv, `docs/context-efficiency-playbook.md` in the Foundry checkout, which is the canonical, fuller version of this specific rule — that entry, not this line, is what to edit first if the rule changes); (3) when a turn's honest answer is one plain sentence (nothing found, nothing left to do), that sentence is the whole response — don't pad it into a question.
- Archived history is deliberately excluded from the SessionStart hook's auto-load, and opening it is opt-in. Don't read an archive speculatively or "for completeness" — that spends the exact context the archive exists to save. Open one only when the current task genuinely needs it: tracing why a decision was later reversed, recovering the original reasoning behind a rule that now looks arbitrary, checking whether an approach was already tried and rejected, or reconstructing what a since-changed requirement used to be. If this project has no archive yet, this rule is dormant rather than satisfied — and when these docs do grow large enough to archive, move the old content **verbatim** (never summarize during a move, so the move itself cannot lose anything) and leave a short pointer block in this file naming each archive and the range it covers. That pointer is the only part guaranteed to load every session, so it is the entire mechanism by which the archive stays findable; an archive nothing points to is indistinguishable from a deleted one. **How you know a pass is due is measured, not noticed:** `/foundry-audit` reports an `auto-load budget` line on every run — the byte size of what loads into every session, which file is largest, and the percentage of budget used — INFO once over budget and FAIL at a hard ceiling. Read the percentage on a passing run, not only the word PASS; that is where growth shows up while it is still cheap to fix. Treat an over-budget INFO as due work, and never clear it by raising the threshold.
